<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
      The app's window chrome. The navigation bar, the window behind Compose and (as
      HomeScreen.kt's ChromeBg) the app's own chrome colour are all #0B0B0D. The status
      bar is transparent: MainActivity turns off decor fitting so the home hero's flag
      is drawn behind it (see WindowCompat.setDecorFitsSystemWindows there), and a
      painted status bar would cut that image off with a hard horizontal seam. Every
      screen keeps its content clear of the bar with statusBarsPadding(), so nothing but
      background art is ever underneath it.

      windowBackground matters even though Compose paints over all of it: it is what
      the system draws for the launch window before the first frame, and left at the
      platform default that was a pale flash ahead of a near-black UI.

      windowLightStatusBar is false, stated rather than inherited. On this background
      the system's glyphs have to be the light set — white on #0B0B0D is 18.4:1, the
      dark set would be about 1.2:1 and effectively invisible — and while the
      platform's dark parent already defaults that way, several OEM skins derive it
      from the phone's own theme instead, which flipped the icons to dark on a
      light-themed device. MainActivity states the same thing at runtime through
      WindowCompat, for both bars, which is also how the navigation bar's glyphs are
      set: windowLightNavigationBar is API 27 and this app ships to 24.
    -->
    <style name="Theme.CDNHunter" parent="android:Theme.Material.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">#FF0B0B0D</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">#FF0B0B0D</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">false</item>
    </style>
</resources>
